This paper introduces iOCR, a novel method for extracting totals from scanned election ballots. Unlike traditional Optical Character Recognition (OCR) systems that rely on pre-defined character sets, iOCR leverages the inherent structure of ballot totals to enhance recognition accuracy. The approach involves two primary stages: first, it identifies the total line by detecting the specific pattern of the total label, and second, it extracts the total value using a custom-trained OCR model. This model is specifically designed to recognize the total label and the corresponding value, which is typically a sequence of digits. The system was evaluated on 1,000 ballots from the 2020 U.S. Presidential election, achieving an accuracy of 99.9% for the total label and 99.8% for the total value. Furthermore, the model demonstrated robustness against common scanning artifacts, including noise, skew, and blur. The source code for iOCR is publicly available at https://github.com/abhishekgupta1999/iOCR.
