We introduce a novel algorithm for the distributed contextual linear bandit problem that operates without sharing the context vector. This approach is applicable to both fully decentralized and partially decentralized network topologies. The algorithm achieves a regret bound of $O(d\sqrt{T})$, where $d$ represents the dimension of the context vector and $T$ denotes the time horizon. This performance matches the optimal regret bound for the centralized setting. Furthermore, we establish a lower bound of $\Omega(d\sqrt{T})$ for the regret in the fully decentralized scenario, demonstrating that our algorithm is optimal up to constant factors. We also provide a numerical evaluation of the algorithm's performance.
